  "mathematics": {
    "scope": "Complete original Chapter20 recursive source read372 lines and first complete revised read371 lines, four author inputs. Earlier Chapter1,6,8,19 inputs retain their recorded current-cycle scopes. The explicitly cited SGPT semigroup theorem, Moishezon projective-modification theorem and Hartogs extension remain classical inputs rather than newly independently reproved here. Later Chapter33 history citation retains its provenance scope.",
    "fresh_checks": [
      "The recursive flag algorithm removes generic divisorial vanishing before each restriction, so each residual section is nonzero. Adapted local coordinates identify its vector with the lexicographically least Taylor exponent.",
      "Valuations add under nonzero products; for equal-degree sums their least exponents cannot cancel when distinct. Products of nonzero sections remain nonzero on connected X.",
      "Distinct valuation vectors give linear independence by the least-term argument. Conversely lexicographic order on N^n has a least value, its coefficient is a nonzero linear functional, and induction on its codimension-one kernel gives exactly dim V values, including V=0.",
      "At stage i the residual bundle retains every prior divisor twist restricted from its own ambient Y_(j-1), with the displayed negative signs.",
      "Pairing the effective residual zero divisor with gamma_i^(d_i-1) yields a_i nu_i <= m A_i - sum B_ij nu_j; each a_i is positive, and a_n=1 for the final point.",
      "The recursively defined nonnegative C_i with absolute values of A_i and B_ij dominates the degree inequality for every section and every m, without requiring any sign of those individual degrees.",
      "The graded algebra makes the section-value semigroup additive. Its linear bound gives a closed containing cone with compact height-one slice and only the origin at height zero.",
      "The closed convex hull of normalized positive-degree values is the height-one slice of the generated closed cone; the entirely zero positive-degree algebra has the specified empty body.",
      "One-dimensional leaves identify each degree cardinality with dim W_m. Disjoint half-open lattice cubes of side 1/m lie in Delta+[0,1/m]^n; the compact neighborhoods decrease to Delta, giving the limsup volume bound even for lower-dimensional or empty bodies.",
      "The primitive lattice equality invokes the explicitly stated SGPT semigroup theorem with all its containing-cone, height-zero and generated-group hypotheses; that external counting theorem is retained rather than independently reproved.",
      "For nonzero positive degrees, the projection of the generated group is eZ, with e their gcd; its horizontal kernel Lambda is a rank-d lattice, with covolume-one measure and the point-volume convention at d=0.",
      "A group element g0=(b,e) and a basis of Lambda give unique integral coordinates G=Z^(d+1); g0 need not itself belong to the positive semigroup.",
      "Normalized positive-degree semigroup elements linearly span span_R G and lie at height one, so their affine span, and hence the body, has dimension d.",
      "The group coordinate change preserves a compact containing cone and the height-zero condition. Its degree q corresponds exactly to original degree eq, and its slice is e(Delta-b/e) in the Lambda basis.",
      "The transformed slice volume is e^d times intrinsic Lambda-volume. Applying the full-lattice counting theorem and dividing by e^d yields the denominator (eq)^d and a finite positive coefficient.",
      "No degree outside eZ can be nonzero. Positivity of the limit gives eventual nonvanishing on eZ; when d=0 the integer counts converge to one and are eventually one.",
      "The section-ratio set is a field: addition and multiplication use products of denominators in degree m1+m2, inversion swaps numerator and denominator, and W0=C supplies constants.",
      "Affinely independent normalized section values can be moved to one common positive degree by powers. Their ratios have linearly independent valuation vectors, and distinct polynomial monomials have distinct values, proving algebraic independence and transcendence degree at least d.",
      "Algebraically independent r ratios admit one nonzero common denominator B in positive degree M. Multiplying every monomial of total degree at most q by B^q gives binomial(q+r,r) independent sections in degree Mq.",
      "The degree M is divisible by e, and the established periodic limit bounds dim W_(Mq) by O(q^d); the binomial lower bound therefore forces r<=d, including exclusion of r>0 when d=0.",
      "The section-ratio field and nonzero degrees do not depend on the flag, determining d and e. The same graded-dimension limit determines intrinsic volume, while body shape and embedded lattices may differ.",
      "A Kahler modification has smooth semipositive pi*omega dominated by C kappa. The smooth-potential BMP criterion from Chapter6 reduces the assertion to those masses.",
      "Top-power comparison follows from a semipositive smooth difference. Change of variables ignores exceptional analytic sets for smooth top-degree forms, and closed kappa plus Stokes makes the dominating mass C^n integral kappa^n independent of u.",
      "Nonempty ambient interior would give n algebraically independent meromorphic functions; the retained classical Moishezon projective-modification theorem then supplies a Kahler modification and BMP. Its contrapositive gives zero body volume and o(m^n) growth on BMP-failing X.",
      "A point blowup for n>=2 supplies a compact smooth flag through the exceptional P^(n-1). Hartogs extends every pulled-back bundle coefficient across the point; identity on the dense isomorphism locus gives inverse pullback and product compatibility. For n=1 the identity and a point suffice.",
      "Pullback preserves the original graded dimensions, nonzero degrees and ratio field. Intrinsic counting on the auxiliary flag consequently gives choice-independent d,e,V for every connected compact complex manifold.",
      "The full section ring either has no positive-degree pieces or has periodic O(m^d) growth, d<=n. Non-Moishezon X forces d<n; BMP failure implies non-Moishezon by the proved modification bound, giving the stated corollary.",
      "Multiplier-ideal section spaces inherit upper bounds by inclusion. Their identification across modifications retains the Chapter1 relative-canonical/Jacobian correction and is not identified with the unweighted Hartogs isomorphism.",
      "On the scalar Hopf flag, successive coordinate restrictions leave a nonzero z_n power on the elliptic fiber, so the final point valuation is zero and preceding nonnegative entries sum to at most km. Every allowed tuple is realized by its displayed homogeneous monomial.",
      "The Hopf normalized values include all simplex vertices already in degree one. For integer k>0, the horizontal group is the full coordinate lattice, e=1, and the (n-1)-volume is k^(n-1)/(n-1)!, matching the Chapter19 binomial coefficient; for k=0 the body is a point and every count is one.",
      "The Hopf threefold drawing labels a triangle in x3=0 with intercepts k, intrinsic area k^2/2 and ambient volume zero; the axes, caption and proved formulas agree. Original page548 is directly inspected and lacks the automatic proof-end square; one blank line immediately before end{proof} is removed for the rebuilt verification. The clean production rebuild restores exactly one proof-end square on page548 after the blank-line removal; the entire final page and figure are directly inspected."
